<section xml:id="isosceles-triangle-angles">
  <title>3.1.4 Convenient Euclid Parallel Axiom</title>

  <proof>
    <p>
      Consider two lines with a transversal. Let the angles on one side of
      the transversal be labeled <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, with <m>C</m> being
      supplementary to <m>B</m>.
    </p>

    <image src="3.1.4.svg">

    <p>
      Suppose
      <me>
        mA + mB = 180^\circ.
      </me>
      Since <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> are supplementary,
      <me>
        mB + mC = 180^\circ.
      </me>
      Therefore,
      <me>
        mA + mB = mB + mC.
      </me>
      Subtracting <m>mB</m> from both sides gives
      <me>
        mA = mC.
      </me>
      Therefore, <m>A</m> and <m>C</m> are congruent alternate interior
      angles. By Theorem 3.1.2 (Alternate Interior Angles), 
      the lines are parallel.
    </p>
  </proof>
</section>


